{"text": "Apple says its biggest business risks are macroeconomic weakness, trade and geopolitical disruption, business interruptions including natural disasters and cyber/ransomware events, intense competition, and intellectual-property/regulatory pressure. In the filing, it says its operations and performance depend significantly on global and regional economic conditions; that trade restrictions and geopolitical tensions can disrupt its supply chain and sales; that its markets are highly competitive with aggressive pricing and margin pressure; and that regulation, government investigations and litigation can force it to withdraw or modify products and services [f_b548ef8ca4d9] [f_fe8c62b61887].

On concentration, Apple does not file a product revenue split in the passages the desk pulled here. What it does say is that it manages the business primarily on a geographic basis, with reportable segments of Americas, Europe, Greater China, Japan and Rest of Asia Pacific, and that sales outside the U.S. represent a majority of total net sales [f_ae2c1edaf470] [f_3bc3eb780139]. It also says 2025 net sales through direct and indirect channels were 40% and 60% of total net sales, respectively [f_ae2c1edaf470].

The desk does not hold product- or geography-concentration figures for AAPL as figures in the fundamentals set. It holds the standard financial fundamentals instead — revenue, margins, cash flow, debt, working capital and the like — but not a product mix or geographic revenue table as a numeric figure [r_519230b658ac]."}

{"text": "Over the last three fiscal years, Amazon spends the most relative to revenue: capex intensity moved from 9.17% [f_dfdbf460f91c] to 13.0% [f_dfdbf460f91c] to 18.4% [f_dfdbf460f91c], versus Alphabet at 10.5% [f_1b205b8af43b] to 15.0% [f_1b205b8af43b] to 22.7% [f_1b205b8af43b] and Microsoft at 13.3% [f_d7ec6444abbf] to 18.1% [f_d7ec6444abbf] to 22.9% [f_d7ec6444abbf]. On ROIC, Microsoft earns the most on its capital in this set, at 32.8% [f_e58c71c6a77a], 29.7% [f_e58c71c6a77a] and 29.7% [f_e58c71c6a77a], while Amazon earns the least across the three, at 15.2% [f_2d0a98adaab6], 22.3% [f_2d0a98adaab6] and 16.2% [f_2d0a98adaab6].

So the clean read is: Amazon is the highest capex-intensity name, and Amazon is also the weakest ROIC name over the three-year window. Microsoft is the highest-ROIC name, even though its capex intensity is the highest in the latest year.

On the portfolio side, the desk does not hold a QQQ factor row for this book; the available factor table shows growth instead, not QQQ, and QQQ is absent [f_4e76e918c595]. That means I can say the book is exposed to the growth factor the desk does hold, but I cannot state a quoted QQQ factor figure from the record. The implication is that this portfolio’s growth tilt is aligned with the same broad regime QQQ represents, so the Amazon/Alphabet/MSFT capex-and-ROIC mix matters more through growth exposure than through a direct QQQ number: high-capex, lower-ROIC names are the more delicate part of that growth exposure, while Microsoft is the more efficient capital compounder inside it.", "problems": [{"rule": 1, "reason": "ambiguous_point", "sentence": "S2"}, {"rule": 1, "reason": "ambiguous_point", "sentence": "S2"}, {"rule": 2, "reason": "superlative_without_rank", "sentence": "S1"}, {"rule": 2, "reason": "superlative_without_rank", "sentence": "S2"}]}

